/**
@page api_deadline_scheduling Soft Deadline Support and Deadline-Based Scheduling
@brief Deadline-based scheduling strategies (EDD/EDF) and soft deadline modeling

This module provides support for modeling soft deadlines (due dates) in queueing systems
and scheduling strategies that prioritize jobs based on their deadlines.

@section deadline_overview Overview

Soft deadlines allow modeling systems where jobs have target completion times (due dates),
but missing a deadline does not result in job termination. This differs from hard deadlines
where jobs are dropped or canceled when deadlines are missed.

LINE supports deadline-based scheduling through:
- **EDD (Earliest Due Date)**: Non-preemptive priority scheduling based on absolute deadlines
- **EDF (Earliest Deadline First)**: Non-preemptive priority scheduling based on absolute deadlines
- **Soft deadline modeling**: Jobs have associated deadline values used by schedulers

@section deadline_vs_impatience Deadlines vs. Impatience

While both deadlines and impatience model time-based constraints on jobs, they differ:

<table>
<tr><th>Feature</th><th>Soft Deadlines</th><th>Impatience (Reneging)</th></tr>
<tr><td>Purpose</td><td>Target completion time for scheduling</td><td>Maximum waiting time before abandonment</td></tr>
<tr><td>Behavior</td><td>Influences scheduling priority</td><td>Job leaves queue if patience expires</td></tr>
<tr><td>Job outcome</td><td>Job completes (may miss deadline)</td><td>Job abandons and leaves system</td></tr>
<tr><td>Classification</td><td>ImpatienceType.DEADLINE</td><td>ImpatienceType.RENEGING or BALKING</td></tr>
</table>

The `ImpatienceType` enum classifies different types of time-based behaviors:
- `ImpatienceType.DEADLINE` - Soft deadlines for scheduling
- `ImpatienceType.RENEGING` - Customer abandonment after waiting
- `ImpatienceType.BALKING` - Customer refusal to join based on queue state

@section deadline_schedulers Deadline-Based Schedulers

@subsection edd_scheduler EDD (Earliest Due Date)

EDD is a non-preemptive priority scheduling strategy that selects the job with the earliest
absolute deadline for service. Jobs are ordered by their deadline values.

<b>Properties:</b>
- Non-preemptive (jobs complete once started)
- Priority class: NPPrio (Non-Preemptive Priority)
- Minimizes maximum lateness in single-server systems
- Optimal for minimizing maximum lateness (non-preemptive case)

<b>Solver Support:</b> JMT, LDES (via SSJ simulation)

@subsection edf_scheduler EDF (Earliest Deadline First)

EDF is conceptually similar to EDD in the non-preemptive case supported by LINE.
Both select jobs with the earliest deadline.

<b>Properties:</b>
- Non-preemptive in LINE's implementation
- Priority class: NPPrio (Non-Preemptive Priority)
- Commonly used in real-time systems

<b>Solver Support:</b> JMT, LDES (via SSJ simulation)

<b>Note:</b> In LINE's current implementation, EDD and EDF are functionally equivalent
as both implement non-preemptive earliest-deadline scheduling. In broader scheduling
literature, EDF often refers to preemptive variants.

@section deadline_usage Usage

@subsection deadline_matlab MATLAB

<b>Setting Deadlines on Job Classes:</b>
@code
% Create open class with deadline
urgentClass = OpenClass(model, 'Urgent', 0);
urgentClass.setDeadline(5.0);  % Deadline of 5 time units

% Or specify deadline in constructor
urgentClass = OpenClass(model, 'Urgent', 0, 5.0);

% Create closed class with deadline
batchClass = ClosedClass(model, 'Batch', 10, node);
batchClass.setDeadline(20.0);
@endcode

<b>Creating Queue with Deadline Scheduling:</b>
@code
% EDD scheduling
queue = Queue(model, 'Server', SchedStrategy.EDD);

% EDF scheduling
queue = Queue(model, 'Server', SchedStrategy.EDF);
@endcode

<b>Complete Example:</b>
@code
model = Network('DeadlineExample');

% Define nodes
source = Source(model, 'Source');
queue = Queue(model, 'Server', SchedStrategy.EDD);
sink = Sink(model, 'Sink');

% Define job classes with different deadlines
urgentClass = OpenClass(model, 'Urgent', 0);
urgentClass.setDeadline(5.0);   % Tight deadline

regularClass = OpenClass(model, 'Regular', 0);
regularClass.setDeadline(15.0);  % Loose deadline

% Set arrival and service distributions
source.setArrival(urgentClass, Exp(0.3));
source.setArrival(regularClass, Exp(0.5));
queue.setService(urgentClass, Exp(2.0));
queue.setService(regularClass, Exp(2.0));

% Link network
model.link(Network.serialRouting(source, queue, sink));

% Solve
solver = SolverJMT(model);
results = solver.getAvgTable();
@endcode

@subsection deadline_java Java/Kotlin

<b>Setting Deadlines on Job Classes:</b>
@code
// Create open class with deadline
OpenClass urgentClass = new OpenClass(model, "Urgent", 0);
urgentClass.setDeadline(5.0);  // Deadline of 5 time units

// Or use constructor
OpenClass urgentClass = new OpenClass(model, "Urgent", 0, 5.0);

// Create closed class with deadline
ClosedClass batchClass = new ClosedClass(model, "Batch", 10, node);
batchClass.setDeadline(20.0);
@endcode

<b>Creating Queue with Deadline Scheduling:</b>
@code
// EDD scheduling
Queue queue = new Queue(model, "Server", SchedStrategy.EDD);

// EDF scheduling
Queue queue = new Queue(model, "Server", SchedStrategy.EDF);
@endcode

<b>Complete Example:</b>
@code
Network model = new Network("DeadlineExample");

// Define nodes
Source source = new Source(model, "Source");
Queue queue = new Queue(model, "Server", SchedStrategy.EDD);
Sink sink = new Sink(model, "Sink");

// Define job classes with different deadlines
OpenClass urgentClass = new OpenClass(model, "Urgent", 0, 5.0);   // Tight deadline
OpenClass regularClass = new OpenClass(model, "Regular", 0, 15.0); // Loose deadline

// Set arrival and service distributions
source.setArrival(urgentClass, new Exp(0.3));
source.setArrival(regularClass, new Exp(0.5));
queue.setService(urgentClass, new Exp(2.0));
queue.setService(regularClass, new Exp(2.0));

// Link network
model.link(Network.serialRouting(source, queue, sink));

// Solve
SolverJMT solver = new SolverJMT(model);
solver.getAvgTable();
@endcode

@subsection deadline_python Python

<b>Setting Deadlines on Job Classes:</b>
@code
# Create open class with deadline
urgent_class = OpenClass(model, 'Urgent', prio=0, deadline=5.0)

# For existing class
urgent_class.set_deadline(5.0)

# Create closed class with deadline
batch_class = ClosedClass(model, 'Batch', population=10, refstat=node, deadline=20.0)
@endcode

<b>Creating Queue with Deadline Scheduling:</b>
@code
# EDD scheduling
queue = Queue(model, 'Server', SchedStrategy.EDD)

# EDF scheduling
queue = Queue(model, 'Server', SchedStrategy.EDF)
@endcode

<b>Complete Example:</b>
@code
from line_solver import *

model = Network('DeadlineExample')

# Define nodes
source = Source(model, 'Source')
queue = Queue(model, 'Server', SchedStrategy.EDD)
sink = Sink(model, 'Sink')

# Define job classes with different deadlines
urgent_class = OpenClass(model, 'Urgent', prio=0, deadline=5.0)   # Tight deadline
regular_class = OpenClass(model, 'Regular', prio=0, deadline=15.0) # Loose deadline

# Set arrival and service distributions
source.setArrival(urgent_class, Exp(0.3))
source.setArrival(regular_class, Exp(0.5))
queue.setService(urgent_class, Exp(2.0))
queue.setService(regular_class, Exp(2.0))

# Link network
model.link(Network.serialRouting(source, queue, sink))

# Solve
solver = SolverJMT(model)
results = solver.getAvgTable()
@endcode

@section deadline_api API Reference

@subsection deadline_jobclass JobClass Methods

<b>setDeadline(double deadline)</b> - Set soft deadline for job class

<b>Syntax:</b>
@code
jobClass.setDeadline(deadline)  % MATLAB
jobClass.setDeadline(deadline);  // Java
job_class.set_deadline(deadline)  # Python
@endcode

<b>Parameters:</b>
<table>
<tr><th>Name</th><th>Type</th><th>Description</th></tr>
<tr><td>deadline</td><td>double</td><td>Target completion time (soft deadline)</td></tr>
</table>

<b>getDeadline()</b> - Get deadline for job class

<b>Syntax:</b>
@code
dl = jobClass.getDeadline()  % MATLAB
double dl = jobClass.getDeadline();  // Java
dl = job_class.get_deadline()  # Python
@endcode

@subsection deadline_schedstrategy SchedStrategy Constants

Available deadline-based scheduling strategies:

<b>MATLAB/Java/Kotlin:</b>
@code
SchedStrategy.EDD  % Earliest Due Date
SchedStrategy.EDF  % Earliest Deadline First
@endcode

<b>Python:</b>
@code
SchedStrategy.EDD  # Earliest Due Date
SchedStrategy.EDF  # Earliest Deadline First
@endcode

@section deadline_examples Examples

<b>Example Files:</b>
- `python/examples/example_deadline.py` - Python example with EDD/EDF scheduling
- `matlab/test/test_forwarding.m` - MATLAB test cases including deadline examples
- `jar/src/test/java/jline/solvers/jmt/SolverJMTTest.java` - Java test cases

@section deadline_references References

- Liu, C. L., & Layland, J. W. (1973). Scheduling algorithms for multiprogramming in a hard-real-time environment. Journal of the ACM, 20(1), 46-61.
- Pinedo, M. L. (2016). Scheduling: Theory, Algorithms, and Systems (5th ed.). Springer.

@see @ref api_impatience for customer abandonment (reneging/balking)
@see ImpatienceType for classification of time-based behaviors
*/
